<GuideArticlePage
  title="Why Is My Watch Losing Time? | Shelton Watch Services"
  description="Why mechanical watches lose time — magnetism, lubrication, regulation, and when slow running means service."
  h1="Why Is My Watch Losing Time?"
  answer="Mechanical watches usually drift when friction rises, magnetism pulls the hairspring out of shape, or regulation no longer compensates for wear — position changes can make a watch look ‘fine’ dial-up but slow crown-left. Quartz pieces rarely ‘lose time’ gradually without a deeper issue; they tend to stop or stutter."
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHref="/service/full-overhaul/"
  ctaLabel="Explore full overhaul service →"
>
  <p>
    Slow running is often progressive. In a sampling mindset, roughly <strong>15–30 seconds per day</strong>{' '}
    might feel tolerable — until you miss a train connection. Track drift over a week, note positions, and
    bring that log to evaluation; it speeds diagnosis.
  </p>
  <h2>How Does Magnetism Show Up in Real Life?</h2>
  <p>
    Speakers, laptops, bag clasps, and tool drawers can magnetize a watch in ordinary routines. The classic
    clue is erratic rates — fast sometimes, slow sometimes — or sudden changes after travel. A coil-style
    demagnetizer is not a toy; if you are not experienced, let a watchmaker confirm.
  </p>
  <h2>When Does Losing Time Mean an Overhaul?</h2>
  <p>
    When amplitude drops and rate stability disappears across positions, oil is often done doing its job.
    Regulation alone becomes lipstick on wear — the movement needs cleaning, inspection, and fresh
    lubrication where pivots demand it.
  </p>
  <h2>What Should You Do Next?</h2>
  <p>
    Book timing evaluation — especially if the watch is a daily wearer you rely on. Learn more about{' '}
    <a href="/service/mechanical-watch/">mechanical watch service</a> and the broader{' '}
    <a href="/service/">service hub</a> for pricing context before you commit.
  </p>
</GuideArticlePage>
